{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy will end as a new law, HB4002, takes effect, re-criminalizing small-scale possession of hard drugs like fentanyl and heroin. Those caught can choose between charges or treatment, including behavioral programs, to avoid fines. Possession is now a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near public spaces like parks. It encourages, but doesn't require, counties to establish treatment alternatives, allowing them to tailor programs to their resources. However, critics argue this may lead to inconsistent policies across different areas.\n\n***\n\nTo qualify for deflection into treatment, individuals must meet strict criteria: no other charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and being medically stable. If eligible, police contact a county dispatch line, which connects them with a mobile behavioral health team that must respond within 30 minutes, or else the individual will be jailed.\n\n***\n\nThe law's backers see the flexibility as beneficial, while opponents foresee a confusing landscape. Portland's police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the narrow criteria and the tight 30-minute response window for treatment teams, emphasizing the consequences of missing this deadline.\n\nP.P.S. As people of all age groups in this country navigate the implications of this law, many might find it hard to eat their meals worrying about the impact. A trip to the shopping mall, once a casual outing, now feels ominous under the new regulations, leaving a bitter taste on one's tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.10877625644207001}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.317